WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.520
At the end of WW1, one of the new countries that&nbsp;
emerged from the ashes of the Austro-Hungarian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:10.020
Empire was Czechoslovakia. The way this&nbsp;
happened was extremely complicated, so if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:14.160
you want to know the background to this history&nbsp;
go watch my breakup of Austria-Hungary videos&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:19.260
and my making of a Czechoslovak state video.
This new country of Czechoslovakia was quite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:24.240
multiethnic but more importantly for this&nbsp;
video, it had a very sizable German minority&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:29.040
that had lived alongside the Czechoslovaks&nbsp;
in the Crownlands of Bohemia and Northern&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:35.280
Hungary for centuries. However, today there are&nbsp;
almost no German speakers to be found in Czechia&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:42.600
or Slovakia, so what happened to the roughly 3.2&nbsp;
million Germans of Czechoslovakia? Let’s find out.

00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:47.760
This video is part of a greater collaboration with&nbsp;
other historytubers called Project Homecoming 2,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:52.260
where everyone makes a history video about&nbsp;
a place where they’re from or where they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:56.340
live or lived. So after this video go&nbsp;
check out all the other historytuber&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.340 --> 00:01:01.380
videos link to the collab playlist is in&nbsp;
the description and the pinned comment.

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:07.140
In the 1920s, once the dust from&nbsp;
the breakup of Austria-Hungary had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:11.520
settled and Czechoslovakia had firmly been&nbsp;
established as a country in Central Europe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:17.940
the new state had one major glaring problem.&nbsp;
Its multiethnic character. Well, at least&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.940 --> 00:01:23.340
that’s how some parts of the population saw it.&nbsp;
In reality, talking about some unified German,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:28.680
Czechoslovak, or even Ruthenian fronts in&nbsp;
Czechoslovakia would be very inaccurate.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:34.380
Movements within these ethnic groups may have seen&nbsp;
themselves as similar in language and culture,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.380 --> 00:01:40.740
but politically the Germans were just as divided&nbsp;
as the Czechoslovaks or Ruthenians. Issues of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:47.040
ethnic sovereignty were as much debated as ideas&nbsp;
of socialism, republicanism, authoritarianism,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.580 --> 00:01:53.820
etc. In essence, the ethnic strife between the&nbsp;
various ‘nations’ within Czechoslovakia was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.820 --> 00:01:59.640
certainly a point of contention, but it wasn’t the&nbsp;
only point of contention. In fact, to some people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:05.100
like socialists and communists, it wasn’t even the&nbsp;
most important point of contention. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:11.400
in the 1920 election, the Hungarian-German Social&nbsp;
Democratic Party, which already had Hungarians and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:16.260
Germans in it, ran on a joint ticket with the&nbsp;
Czechoslovak Social Democratic Workers Party,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.260 --> 00:02:20.700
which was the largest political party in&nbsp;
Czechoslovakia at the time. During their political&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.700 --> 00:02:26.280
campaign in Bratislava, their banners had three&nbsp;
different languages on them, all with various&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:32.460
socialist slogans like ‘death to the profiteers’.&nbsp;
At the same time, the Magyar-German pro-communist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.460 --> 00:02:38.160
labour conference in Košice called for the&nbsp;
interethnic unity of all Czechoslovak workers.&nbsp;

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:43.500
Clearly, to some politicians and voters in&nbsp;
early Czechoslovakia, the ethnic problem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:49.680
wasn’t that relevant. Their perceived economic,&nbsp;
social and class struggles were, for them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:56.280
the priority. However, some doesn’t mean all, and&nbsp;
considering that ethnocentric nationalism was at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.280 --> 00:03:01.920
the heart of Czechoslovakia’s push for national&nbsp;
sovereignty after WW1, it would be far-fetched&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:08.520
to think that ethnic tensions weren’t dominant in&nbsp;
interwar Czechoslovakia. And, it’s these ethnic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.520 --> 00:03:14.340
tensions that eventually lead to ‘one of the&nbsp;
largest waves of forced migration in history’.

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:21.300
In this video, I will discuss the 20th-century&nbsp;
history of only Czechoslovak Germans because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.300 --> 00:03:26.460
their history differs from that of other minority&nbsp;
groups of Czechoslovakia, like Hungarians and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.460 --> 00:03:32.100
Ruthenians. Perhaps I will discuss them in another&nbsp;
video in the future. I will also talk about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:37.740
Czechoslovaks in the interwar period as one single&nbsp;
national group because most (but not all) official&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.740 --> 00:03:42.540
Czechoslovak statistics and documentation of&nbsp;
the time didn’t differentiate between Czechs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.540 --> 00:03:48.420
and Slovaks, and, for the most part, in the first&nbsp;
Czechoslovak Republic, the Czechs and Slovaks were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.420 --> 00:03:54.900
classified as one single nation composed of two&nbsp;
national subgroups. The Slovak Declaration of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.900 --> 00:04:00.840
Independence in Martin even explicitly stated&nbsp;
it. Plus, except for a brief period during WW2,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.840 --> 00:04:05.220
everything in this video was done equally&nbsp;
by both the Czechs and the Slovaks.

00:04:05.220 --> 00:04:10.197
--------------------------Forming an Accord&nbsp;
in the New State------------------------

00:04:10.197 --> 00:04:15.240
In 1918, when the fate and exact borders&nbsp;
of the new country were still uncertain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.240 --> 00:04:21.240
most German-speaking towns within the historical&nbsp;
borders of the Bohemian Kingdom and Northern&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.240 --> 00:04:26.940
Hungary declared themselves independent and&nbsp;
worked towards joining either Austria, Germany,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.940 --> 00:04:33.360
or Hungary. The Zipser Saxons of today’s Spiš&nbsp;
region of Slovakia declared independence and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.360 --> 00:04:38.100
unification with Hungary, the Germans and&nbsp;
Hungarians of Pressburg declared the city&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.100 --> 00:04:43.860
independent and neutral, and the various Sudeten&nbsp;
Germans declared independence and unification with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.860 --> 00:04:49.500
either Austria or Germany. In fact, in the brief&nbsp;
moment when Austria-Hungary had fallen apart,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.500 --> 00:04:54.240
but Germany had still not surrendered, there&nbsp;
existed German-sponsored bases all around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:59.820
Czechoslovakia where radical Czechoslovak Germans&nbsp;
were armed and trained to fight the fledgling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.820 --> 00:05:06.060
Czechoslovak government. In the end, however,&nbsp;
Germany, Austria, and Hungary failed in contesting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.060 --> 00:05:11.880
the Czechoslovak takeover of the German-majority&nbsp;
populated areas allocated to it in the Paris peace&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.880 --> 00:05:16.980
conference. This success of Czechoslovakia in&nbsp;
taking over the majority of German-speaking land&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.980 --> 00:05:22.980
can be primarily attributed to Entente support&nbsp;
and the fact that most Germans in Czechoslovakia&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.980 --> 00:05:29.100
had extreme war weariness after WW1 and promoted&nbsp;
peaceful resistance rather than a military one.

00:05:29.100 --> 00:05:33.900
That is why despite the anti-Czechoslovak&nbsp;
sentiments in many of the German-majority&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.900 --> 00:05:39.720
areas of Czechoslovakia, the takeover of those&nbsp;
areas by Czechoslovak troops was largely peaceful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.720 --> 00:05:45.720
in terms of human cost. Apart from riots and a&nbsp;
couple of deaths here and there due to attacks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.720 --> 00:05:51.060
by more radical Germans and Czechoslovaks, there&nbsp;
was only one major violent clash between the two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.060 --> 00:05:56.460
groups. Well, I say ‘clash between the two&nbsp;
groups’, but in reality, it was very much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.460 --> 00:06:02.220
a one-sided affair. Shortly after Czechoslovak&nbsp;
troops fully occupied the Sudeten German area,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.220 --> 00:06:08.160
a protest was scheduled by the Germans on&nbsp;
March 4th 1919, across the various Sudeten&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.160 --> 00:06:13.500
German towns and villages. The Sudeten Germans&nbsp;
were demanding a vote for self-determination.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.500 --> 00:06:19.800
The protesters were unarmed, but as already&nbsp;
discussed, some radicalised Germans conducted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.800 --> 00:06:24.180
‘semi-military kind’ activities in the&nbsp;
area and some Czechoslovak soldiers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.180 --> 00:06:30.600
fearing the protesters might have weapons, opened&nbsp;
fire in several towns and villages. In total,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.600 --> 00:06:36.960
54 German protesters were killed and 84&nbsp;
wounded. This event was no doubt a tragedy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.960 --> 00:06:42.720
but it paled in comparison to other very bloody&nbsp;
battles happening all across the former lands&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.720 --> 00:06:48.240
of Austria-Hungary, in Slovakia, in Poland,&nbsp;
in Slovenia, etc. In fact, the occupation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.240 --> 00:06:54.180
of German-majority areas by Czechoslovak troops&nbsp;
can be considered fairly peaceful on both sides.

00:06:54.180 --> 00:06:58.980
The fairly peaceful occupation of the&nbsp;
majority German-populated areas is one thing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.980 --> 00:07:04.800
but why was Czechoslovakia even given the majority&nbsp;
German areas in the Paris peace conference? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.800 --> 00:07:09.120
as always, there are multiple reasons&nbsp;
for it, but the simplified version is:

00:07:09.780 --> 00:07:13.320
First, Czechoslovakia was allied&nbsp;
with the Entente during the war.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.320 --> 00:07:17.580
Czechoslovak legions fought on the&nbsp;
side of the Entente in the war. Hence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.580 --> 00:07:21.900
just like France got the German majority&nbsp;
Alsace–Lorraine and Italy got the German&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.900 --> 00:07:26.940
majority Southern Tyrol, Czechoslovakia got&nbsp;
some German-majority parts of Austria-Hungary.

00:07:26.940 --> 00:07:32.340
Second, the Czechoslovak movement was very&nbsp;
adamant about preserving the historical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.340 --> 00:07:38.520
lands of the Bohemian Crown. The Czechoslovaks&nbsp;
saw all the Bohemian Crown lands as rightfully&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.520 --> 00:07:42.780
theirs regardless of whether more Germans&nbsp;
than Czechoslovaks currently populated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.780 --> 00:07:47.340
some parts of it. The Czechoslovaks&nbsp;
saw the Germans as just immigrants&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.340 --> 00:07:52.800
or colonisers of a land which has always&nbsp;
been rightfully owned by the Czechoslovaks.

00:07:52.800 --> 00:07:59.460
Third, the Entente, especially the French, didn’t&nbsp;
want to create a country that would be too small&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.460 --> 00:08:05.700
and fragile and, therefore, incapable of defending&nbsp;
itself against possible future German, Austrian,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.700 --> 00:08:11.160
Hungarian, or Soviet aggression. In the eyes&nbsp;
of the Entente, either you made an economically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.160 --> 00:08:16.440
robust country with strong natural borders,&nbsp;
or you might as well not make a new country at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.440 --> 00:08:22.020
all because it would just fall to its stronger&nbsp;
neighbours. This is why industrially important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.020 --> 00:08:27.240
areas like northern Bohemia or strategically&nbsp;
important waterways, like the Danube River,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.240 --> 00:08:33.000
were given to Czechoslovakia despite these areas&nbsp;
having a majority non-Czechoslovak populations.

00:08:33.000 --> 00:08:37.980
These were the main reasons why the&nbsp;
majority German-speaking areas were given&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.980 --> 00:08:43.740
to Czechoslovakia in the Paris peace conference.
How was Czechoslovakia to deal with such a large&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.740 --> 00:08:48.300
German minority was a matter of debate&nbsp;
in the Czechoslovak political circles.

00:08:48.300 --> 00:08:53.760
For example, when the first prime minister&nbsp;
of Czechoslovakia, Karel Kramář, learned of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.760 --> 00:08:59.220
some Czechoslovaks violently taking down German&nbsp;
signs and inscriptions in the aftermath of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.220 --> 00:09:05.160
Declaration of Independence, he stated that ‘I&nbsp;
believe it is our duty to say to ourselves that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.160 --> 00:09:11.580
every emphatic insistence upon our victory is&nbsp;
a sin against the future of our state. We must&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.580 --> 00:09:17.220
at last learn consideration for the psychology&nbsp;
of the German people … I am profoundly ashamed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.220 --> 00:09:22.680
when I discovered that German inscriptions are&nbsp;
torn down in a German town.’ At the same time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.680 --> 00:09:28.200
however, some Czechoslovak leaders like&nbsp;
Alois Rašín were far less sympathetic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.200 --> 00:09:32.580
stating that ‘If the Germans insisted upon&nbsp;
having their own independent government’&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.580 --> 00:09:37.560
like they did when they called for a vote&nbsp;
of self-determination ‘then the Czech must&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.560 --> 00:09:43.260
direct their policy in accordance with the old&nbsp;
principle: no negotiations with the rebels!’.

00:09:43.260 --> 00:09:49.980
With that said, Rašín style views were largely in&nbsp;
the minority, and ‘officially there was an effort&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.980 --> 00:09:55.080
to follow from [Czechoslovak] politicians&nbsp;
rejecting chauvinism’. That is why Masarik,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.080 --> 00:09:58.500
and Benes offered to collaborate with&nbsp;
the German and Hungarian minority&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.500 --> 00:09:59.880
parties (for example, like the German Social&nbsp;
Democratic Workers’ Party) in the writing of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.880 --> 00:10:06.180
the Czechoslovak constitution. But, the leaders&nbsp;
of the minority parties refused this offer and, in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.180 --> 00:10:12.180
protest of not being allowed self-determination,&nbsp;
boycotted the Constituent Assembly. What the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.180 --> 00:10:16.860
various German factions actually wanted when&nbsp;
they called for, quote, ‘self-determination’&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.860 --> 00:10:22.800
was quite varied. Some wanted to unite with&nbsp;
Germany, others with Austria or Hungary,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.800 --> 00:10:28.800
some wanted to be completely independent, and some&nbsp;
wanted federal autonomy within Czechoslovakia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.800 --> 00:10:34.020
and as Wiskemann points out, ‘it would be&nbsp;
impossible to estimate proportions’ of which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.020 --> 00:10:39.060
of these solutions were the most popular. He&nbsp;
continues to say that this disunity should not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.060 --> 00:10:44.520
be surprising as ‘The three million Germans who&nbsp;
now found themselves to be citizens of the new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.520 --> 00:10:50.040
Czechoslovak Republic had surprisingly little&nbsp;
in common until they became members of the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.040 --> 00:10:55.860
so-called minority.’ The terms Sudeten Germans,&nbsp;
referring to the German-speaking population of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.860 --> 00:11:00.600
Czechia, and Carpathian Germans, referring to&nbsp;
the German-speaking population of Slovakia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.600 --> 00:11:05.340
weren’t even used in their current definition&nbsp;
before the formation of Czechoslovakia.

00:11:05.340 --> 00:11:10.800
However, for now, even though disagreeing on what&nbsp;
the ‘self-determination’ vote should be about,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.800 --> 00:11:16.380
the various German factions could at least agree&nbsp;
on the boycott of the Constituent Assembly. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.380 --> 00:11:21.780
meant that the constitution of Czechoslovakia&nbsp;
was written without German or Hungarian input.

00:11:21.780 --> 00:11:28.200
After the first parliamentary election in 1920,&nbsp;
in which 6 competing German political parties&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.200 --> 00:11:34.260
won 26% of the seats, the German parties finally&nbsp;
showed up to the parliament where they vehemently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.260 --> 00:11:38.820
complained against the constitution,&nbsp;
which quote ‘had been forced upon them’,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.820 --> 00:11:44.340
despite the fact that they refused to collaborate&nbsp;
in its writing when specifically offered to.

00:11:44.340 --> 00:11:49.560
The primary point of contention for the&nbsp;
Germans was Article 6 of the constitution,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.560 --> 00:11:53.940
devoted to the Protection of National,&nbsp;
Religious and Racial Minorities.&nbsp;

00:11:53.940 --> 00:12:00.000
Its first clause stated that ‘All citizens of the&nbsp;
Czechoslovak Republic shall be in all respects&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:05.040
equal before the law and shall enjoy equal civic&nbsp;
and political rights whatever be their race,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.040 --> 00:12:10.800
their language or their religion … Difference in&nbsp;
religion, belief, confession or language shall&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.800 --> 00:12:15.900
within the limits of the common law constitute&nbsp;
no obstacle to any citizen of the Czechoslovak&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.900 --> 00:12:21.240
Republic particularly in regard of entry into&nbsp;
the public services and offices, of attainment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.240 --> 00:12:27.660
to any promotion or dignity, or in regard to the&nbsp;
exercise of any trade or calling.’ Also, ‘In towns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.660 --> 00:12:32.460
and districts in which there lives a considerable&nbsp;
fraction (20%) of Czechoslovak citizens speaking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:32.460 --> 00:12:37.500
a language other than Czechoslovak, the children&nbsp;
of such Czechoslovak citizens shall, in public&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:37.500 --> 00:12:42.960
instruction and within the bounds of the general&nbsp;
regulations relating thereto, be guaranteed a due&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:42.960 --> 00:12:48.600
opportunity to receive instruction in their own&nbsp;
tongue.’ All deputies of the parliament also had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.600 --> 00:12:53.460
the right to speak in their native language in&nbsp;
parliament, all official Government documents,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:53.460 --> 00:12:56.280
including currency, will have 4 languages on it&nbsp;
(Czechoslovak, Hungarian, Ruthenian, and German),&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.280 --> 00:13:01.560
and lastly, ‘Every manner whatsoever of&nbsp;
forcible denationalisation is prohibited;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:01.560 --> 00:13:06.000
Non-observance of this principle may be&nbsp;
proclaimed by law to be a pu­nishable act.’

00:13:06.000 --> 00:13:12.540
Historian Jan Kuklík, in his book Minorities and&nbsp;
Law in Czechoslovakia, deems the Czechoslovak&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.540 --> 00:13:17.700
Constitution ‘rather temperate and fair from&nbsp;
the point of view of minorities.’ In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:17.700 --> 00:13:22.020
the Czechoslovak constitution was one&nbsp;
of the most progressive on the continent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.040 --> 00:13:27.360
in terms of minority rights, especially&nbsp;
when compared to countries like France.

00:13:27.360 --> 00:13:33.780
So what were the Germans complaining about? Well,&nbsp;
the 20% mark meant that towns and villages which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:33.780 --> 00:13:39.840
had a German population of 19.9% would not have&nbsp;
the legal right to public instruction in their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:39.840 --> 00:13:44.700
own language, nor would the government offices&nbsp;
and courts in those towns and villages have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:44.700 --> 00:13:50.040
allow the use of another language other than&nbsp;
Czechoslovak in their proceedings. This meant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.040 --> 00:13:56.640
that towns and villages with less than 20% German&nbsp;
population would undergo Czechoslovakization. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:56.640 --> 00:14:01.740
ironic part was that the 20% mark wasn’t&nbsp;
made up by the Czechoslovak government.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:01.740 --> 00:14:07.860
It was inherited from the Austro-Hungarian&nbsp;
system. One can’t help but spot the hypocrisy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:07.860 --> 00:14:12.900
20% was good enough in the Austro-Hungarian&nbsp;
system when German and Hungarian were the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:12.900 --> 00:14:17.520
official languages, but it was suddenly too high&nbsp;
when Czechoslovak was the official language.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:17.520 --> 00:14:23.460
Historian Carlile Macartney aptly summarised&nbsp;
the situation by stating, ‘equality would not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:23.460 --> 00:14:28.140
have satisfied the Magyars and Germans, [who&nbsp;
have been] costumed so long to domination.’

00:14:28.140 --> 00:14:33.420
It also must be stressed that the Paris&nbsp;
peace treaties explicitly stated that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:33.420 --> 00:14:38.700
there must be favourable minority laws in the&nbsp;
post-Austro-Hungarian countries. Otherwise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:38.700 --> 00:14:43.800
the Entente would not support their creation.&nbsp;
However, considering most leading Czechoslovak&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:43.800 --> 00:14:48.780
politicians of the time were not of a very&nbsp;
chauvinistic character, the minority laws would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:48.780 --> 00:14:54.300
have probably been written the same whether the&nbsp;
Entente enforced them or not. Most Czechoslovak&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:54.300 --> 00:14:59.400
politicians were not blind to the fact that a&nbsp;
large part of their new country’s population were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:59.400 --> 00:15:05.580
minorities, and they knew antagonising them would&nbsp;
only lead to more problems and instability. That&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:05.580 --> 00:15:10.320
is why initially, despite what the harrumphing&nbsp;
of the Germans and Hungarians in the parliament&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:10.320 --> 00:15:14.700
might make you believe, the Czechoslovak&nbsp;
state was quite lenient towards minorities.

00:15:14.700 --> 00:15:20.460
The most ardent proponent of such leniency was&nbsp;
none other than the first Czechoslovak president&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:20.460 --> 00:15:27.300
Tomáš Masaryk. Himself of multiethnic origin,&nbsp;
Masaryk never really agreed with the idea of an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:27.300 --> 00:15:33.480
ethnostate. He believed the idea of ethnicity or&nbsp;
a ‘nation group’ should be separate from the idea&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:33.480 --> 00:15:39.480
of a state, a country. One is a sense of social&nbsp;
identity, the other is a form of government in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:39.480 --> 00:15:45.120
a particular terriotiry, and in Masaryk’s eyes,&nbsp;
these should not be treated as the same. Masaryk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:45.120 --> 00:15:51.300
was a classicist at heart, and he often echoed&nbsp;
Cicero’s idea of a ‘nation’. According to Cicero&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:51.300 --> 00:15:55.920
‘a nation is not any collection of human&nbsp;
beings brought together in any sort of way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:55.920 --> 00:16:01.740
but an assemblage of people in large numbers&nbsp;
associated in an agreement with respect to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:01.740 --> 00:16:06.660
justice and a partnership for the common&nbsp;
good.’ Austria-Hungary had proved not to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:06.660 --> 00:16:10.440
be a ‘partnership’ for ‘justice’ and&nbsp;
‘common good’ for all its citizens,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:10.440 --> 00:16:16.740
but Czechoslovakia will … well, at least,&nbsp;
that’s what Masaryk hoped. It was, therefore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:16.740 --> 00:16:21.780
with this mindset that Masaryk pardoned&nbsp;
the Germans and Hungarians who took up arms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:21.780 --> 00:16:26.340
against Czechoslovakia during the turbulent&nbsp;
break up of Austria-Hungary, it was with this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:26.340 --> 00:16:30.900
mindset that he argued for lenient minority&nbsp;
laws in the writing of the constitution,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:30.900 --> 00:16:36.120
and it was with this mindset that Masaryk tried&nbsp;
to govern the country during his presidency.

00:16:36.120 --> 00:16:42.000
However, not everyone shared Masaryk’s&nbsp;
ideals, and Czechoslovakia had plenty of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:42.000 --> 00:16:47.340
nationalists unwilling to give ground to the&nbsp;
Germans or other minorities. In the 1920s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:47.340 --> 00:16:52.740
despite the existence of minority laws,&nbsp;
some Czechoslovaks sought to skirt the law&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:52.740 --> 00:16:57.900
wherever they could. For example, building new&nbsp;
Czechoslovak schools in German speaking areas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:57.900 --> 00:17:03.120
was interestingly always approved faster&nbsp;
than building new German schools. Also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:03.120 --> 00:17:07.620
if German-speaking officials wanted to work&nbsp;
in a predominantly Czechoslovak-speaking area,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:07.620 --> 00:17:12.060
they had to take a test in Czechoslovak,&nbsp;
while Czechoslovak officials wanting to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:12.060 --> 00:17:16.740
work in a predominantly German-speaking area&nbsp;
had to declare they knew German but were never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:16.740 --> 00:17:21.540
tested on it. These mandatory Czechoslovak tests&nbsp;
for German officials working in predominantly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:21.540 --> 00:17:27.240
Czechoslovak-speaking areas resulted in the&nbsp;
firing of 33,000 German officials, while the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:27.240 --> 00:17:32.100
Czechoslovak officials deemed not to know German&nbsp;
usually just got reassigned to a different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:32.100 --> 00:17:38.100
area. Such leniencies to Czechoslovak-speaking&nbsp;
people, which were technically never breaking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:38.100 --> 00:17:42.540
the law but could hardly be called equal,&nbsp;
were present everywhere in the country.

00:17:42.540 --> 00:17:48.000
Therefore, it should not be surprising that&nbsp;
‘During the interwar period, many petitions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:48.000 --> 00:17:53.520
were filed against Czechoslovakia with the League&nbsp;
of Nations, usually by the German and to lesser&nbsp;

00:17:53.520 --> 00:17:57.780
extend also by the Hungarian minority. A&nbsp;
greater part of them claimed breaches of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:57.780 --> 00:18:01.740
the Minorities Treaty in connection with&nbsp;
the Czechoslovak Constitution’. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:01.740 --> 00:18:04.740
the League of Nations never&nbsp;
found any major breaches.

00:18:04.740 --> 00:18:10.620
With that said, the 1920s, in general, could&nbsp;
be called a time of accord in Czechoslovakia&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:10.620 --> 00:18:14.280
as the minority parties eventually&nbsp;
accepted they wouldn’t get their vote&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:14.280 --> 00:18:19.860
of self-determination and started to work with&nbsp;
the institutions of their new country. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:19.860 --> 00:18:24.900
some German parties even formed political&nbsp;
coalitions with Czechoslovak parties and had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:24.900 --> 00:18:26.640
ministers in government (Bund der Landwirte&nbsp;
and German Christian Social People’s Party).

00:18:26.640 --> 00:18:31.200
While the 1920s could be called a&nbsp;
time of accord in Czechoslovakia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:31.200 --> 00:18:36.540
the 1930s were far from it; the&nbsp;
1930s were a time of division.

00:18:36.540 --> 00:18:38.460
--------------------------&nbsp;
Nationalsozialismus------------------------

00:18:38.460 --> 00:18:47.280
At the end of WW1, the German&nbsp;
National Socialist Workers’&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:47.280 --> 00:18:51.600
Party (in Austria), also known as the&nbsp;
Nazis for short, was created in Vienna,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:51.600 --> 00:18:55.680
formed by the former members of the German&nbsp;
Workers Party (in Austria). The Nazi party&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:55.680 --> 00:19:01.140
was inspired by Austro-Hungarian politicians&nbsp;
Karl Hermann Wolf and George von Schonerer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:01.140 --> 00:19:07.020
who were part of the late 19th-century and&nbsp;
early 20th-century Pan-German Union, which was a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:07.020 --> 00:19:10.200
coalition party (Also known as the German-National&nbsp;
Association) in Austria. Pan-German Union split&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:10.200 --> 00:19:14.580
into multiple groups, the most notable of which&nbsp;
was the German Workers Party (in Austria), founded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:14.580 --> 00:19:19.200
in Bohemia, from which the aforementioned&nbsp;
German National Socialist Workers’ Party,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:19.200 --> 00:19:25.080
or the Nazis for short, was created. The Nazis&nbsp;
were later forced to split into two parties due&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:25.080 --> 00:19:29.880
to the breakup of Austria-Hungary, the Austrian&nbsp;
Nazi Party and the Czechoslovak Nazi Party.

00:19:29.880 --> 00:19:36.060
Another person who was inspired by Karl Hermann&nbsp;
Wolf, George von Schonerer and their Pan-German&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:36.060 --> 00:19:41.700
Union was Adolf Hitler. He himself joined the&nbsp;
German Workers Party in Germany and pushed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:41.700 --> 00:19:46.380
for its renaming to the National Socialist&nbsp;
German Workers’ Party, also known as Nazis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:46.380 --> 00:19:52.020
for short. In fact, when the German Nazi party&nbsp;
was effectively founded by its renaming in 1920,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:52.020 --> 00:19:58.080
the Czechoslovak Nazis and the Austrian Nazis were&nbsp;
so delighted they sent letters of congratulation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:58.080 --> 00:20:04.020
to the new Munich-based party, and they all&nbsp;
organised a Nazi congress in Salzburg that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:04.020 --> 00:20:08.640
same year. As you may have guessed, all these&nbsp;
parties, including the older Austrian ones,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:08.640 --> 00:20:13.620
were anti-Semitic, anti-democratic,&nbsp;
far-right racist, pan-German parties.

00:20:13.620 --> 00:20:18.300
An interesting side note here. The&nbsp;
original German Workers Party in Austria,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:18.300 --> 00:20:23.940
from which the later Nazi parties formed, was&nbsp;
founded in Bohemia during the Austro-Hungarian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:23.940 --> 00:20:28.680
empire as a radical far-right German&nbsp;
response to the demands of the Czechs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:28.680 --> 00:20:33.480
for more autonomy and more minority rights.&nbsp;
One of the party leaders, Walter Riehl,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:33.480 --> 00:20:39.300
who was later a member of the Nazi party during&nbsp;
WW2, even ‘led [young youths] into the streets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:39.300 --> 00:20:45.600
to fight physically against Czech immigrant&nbsp;
workers’ in 1908 in the Sudetens. Therefore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:45.600 --> 00:20:51.360
as stated by Wiskemann, ironically, ‘Bohemia&nbsp;
was the cradle of German National Socialism.’

00:20:51.360 --> 00:20:57.420
With that said, the Czechoslovak Nazis had far&nbsp;
less success than their German counterparts, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:57.420 --> 00:21:03.660
at most, they only managed to get 3.5% of the vote&nbsp;
in Czechoslovakia. In fact, other German parties&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:03.660 --> 00:21:08.700
in Czechoslovakia blamed them for hindering&nbsp;
their progress toward ethnic equality within&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:08.700 --> 00:21:14.580
the country. However, the Czechoslovak Nazis had&nbsp;
very strong regional backing, with around 60,000&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:14.580 --> 00:21:21.300
radical members in various Sudeten chapters and&nbsp;
an organised paramilitary group called Volkssport,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:21.300 --> 00:21:27.120
which was basically an exact copy of the&nbsp;
German Nazis Storm Detachments. Unsurprisingly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:27.120 --> 00:21:32.880
in the early 1930s, some members of the Volkssport&nbsp;
were plotting with the German Nazis to overthrow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:32.880 --> 00:21:38.280
the Czechoslovak government in order to unite&nbsp;
Czechia with Germany. This plot was discovered,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:38.280 --> 00:21:42.180
and even though it was found to&nbsp;
have no actual plan of execution,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:42.180 --> 00:21:46.260
the perpetrators pled guilty to&nbsp;
conspiring against the state and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:46.260 --> 00:21:51.420
were jailed. Due to this trial, the whole&nbsp;
Czechoslovak Nazi party was banned in 1933.

00:21:51.420 --> 00:21:56.940
The direct military threat presented by the&nbsp;
Czechoslovak Nazis made the Czechoslovak state&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:56.940 --> 00:22:02.760
become far more nationalistic than it had been&nbsp;
prior. German police officers in areas which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:02.760 --> 00:22:08.160
had sizable Nazi support were deemed unreliable&nbsp;
and replaced with Czechoslovak police officers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:08.160 --> 00:22:12.360
and many German ministers simply&nbsp;
advocating for German causes in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:12.360 --> 00:22:15.360
the parliament started to be&nbsp;
viewed as national traitors.

00:22:15.360 --> 00:22:20.640
The increasing nationalism and the frequent&nbsp;
skirting around the minority laws by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:20.640 --> 00:22:24.960
Czechoslovaks pushed a lot of centrist&nbsp;
Germans towards the more radical right,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:24.960 --> 00:22:30.120
but what really sealed the deal was the&nbsp;
Great Depression of the early 1930s.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:30.120 --> 00:22:34.740
Czechslovaks were mostly farmers,&nbsp;
while Germans were mostly artisans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:34.740 --> 00:22:40.260
factory workers, and small business owners,&nbsp;
all sectors of the economy that got hit much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:40.260 --> 00:22:44.520
harder in Czechoslovakia during the great&nbsp;
depression. At its height, the unemployment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:44.520 --> 00:22:50.280
rate of Germans in Czechoslovakia is estimated&nbsp;
to be almost double that of the Czechoslovaks.

00:22:50.280 --> 00:22:55.560
The Great Depression reached its height in&nbsp;
Czechoslovakia in 1933, the same year that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:55.560 --> 00:23:01.200
the Nazi party got banned and a new right-wing&nbsp;
German party was formed, the Sudeten German Party,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:01.200 --> 00:23:05.880
which had multiple names throughout its history,&nbsp;
but we’ll just call it the Sudeten German Party&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:05.880 --> 00:23:10.440
for simplicity. Almost all of the former&nbsp;
supporters and members of the Czechoslovak&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:10.440 --> 00:23:15.900
Nazi party joined the Sudeten German party;&nbsp;
it also gained a lot of new unemployed German&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:15.900 --> 00:23:21.600
supporters who were disgruntled with the current&nbsp;
status quo and wanted to see a drastic change.

00:23:21.600 --> 00:23:28.800
Unlike the Nazi party, the Sudeten German Party&nbsp;
officially adopted a less radical platform. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:28.800 --> 00:23:35.220
key word here is ‘officially’. The leader of the&nbsp;
party Konrad Henlein officially stated that he was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:35.220 --> 00:23:40.620
only fighting for equal rights for the Germans&nbsp;
in Czechoslovakia and that he only wanted more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:40.620 --> 00:23:46.200
autonomy for the Germans within Czechoslovakia&nbsp;
based on either a federal system or a Swiss&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:46.200 --> 00:23:51.900
canton system. This less radical official stance&nbsp;
was because much of the Sudeten German voting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:51.900 --> 00:23:57.300
base at this point didn’t want to break away from&nbsp;
Czechoslovakia, nor were many of them politically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:57.300 --> 00:24:03.480
fascist. A British foreign office official&nbsp;
stationed in Prague in 1934 ‘had come to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:03.480 --> 00:24:09.360
conclusion based on information available that a&nbsp;
demand to revise the borders did not particularly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:09.360 --> 00:24:16.080
resonate amongst Czech Germans.’ That is why the&nbsp;
Sudeten German Party officially adopted a far less&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:16.080 --> 00:24:22.380
radical agenda. While secretly, the Sudeten German&nbsp;
party promoted fascist ideology and was even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:22.380 --> 00:24:28.320
funded by the German Nazis. Henlein, before the&nbsp;
Sudeten German Party was founded, even stated that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:28.320 --> 00:24:34.500
‘We declare war to the death upon liberalism even&nbsp;
behind the disguise of the cult of personality.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:34.500 --> 00:24:40.560
Disciplined mass unities rule the present-&nbsp;
Fascist, the Heimwehr, the Hitler movement. For&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:40.560 --> 00:24:46.920
men wish to be led in manly fashion … We all know&nbsp;
that an un-German parliamentarism, an un-German&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:46.920 --> 00:24:53.280
party system, which divides our people into&nbsp;
inorganic parts, will and must break down at some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:53.280 --> 00:24:59.460
point.’ Henlein said this before the Sudeten Party&nbsp;
was formed, but he never changed his ideology&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:59.460 --> 00:25:05.700
since then. He simply just officially presented&nbsp;
a far less radical political agenda to avoid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:05.700 --> 00:25:12.420
getting banned, but secretly he was financed by&nbsp;
the Nazis and held the same beliefs as the Nazis.

00:25:12.420 --> 00:25:17.580
Despite the backroom fascism of the German&nbsp;
Sudeten party, the breaches of the minority&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:17.580 --> 00:25:22.440
laws by nationalistic Czechoslovaks, the rise&nbsp;
of Nazi Germany and fascism across Europe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:22.440 --> 00:25:27.240
the large unemployed rate in German areas,&nbsp;
and the less radical official stand of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:27.240 --> 00:25:32.040
Sudeten German party, resulted in the&nbsp;
party winning a major political victory&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:32.040 --> 00:25:39.960
in the 1935 election. It gained over 60% of the&nbsp;
German vote, which was 15% of the total vote,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:39.960 --> 00:25:46.140
tying it with the Agrarian Party for the largest&nbsp;
party in the Czechoslovak parliament. It, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:46.140 --> 00:25:51.060
still ended up being in the opposition because&nbsp;
various parties set up a coalition government.

00:25:51.060 --> 00:25:56.880
Once in the parliament, the Sudeten German&nbsp;
party, despite its campaign promises, became&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:56.880 --> 00:26:03.420
increasingly fascist and irredentist. New German&nbsp;
Nazi propaganda started to be distributed in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:03.420 --> 00:26:08.700
Sudetenland, and new paramilitary groups were&nbsp;
organised. In response, the Czechoslovaks became&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:08.700 --> 00:26:13.980
increasingly nationalistic and banned a couple&nbsp;
of German fascist newspapers and imprisoned their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:13.980 --> 00:26:20.040
publishers. Political extremism in Czechoslovakia&nbsp;
became a positive feedback loop, with both sides&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:20.040 --> 00:26:25.200
becoming ever more radical in their actions&nbsp;
and both blaming the other for escalating it.

00:26:25.200 --> 00:26:31.380
Despite all this, a British historian writing&nbsp;
in 1937 stated, ‘Today Czechoslovakia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:31.380 --> 00:26:37.020
almost alone among European countries east of&nbsp;
Switzerland, enjoys a genuine parliamentary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:37.020 --> 00:26:43.080
system of government. There is a franchise which&nbsp;
is equal for all nationalities, a ballot which is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:43.080 --> 00:26:49.620
genuinely secret, reasonable liberty of agitation&nbsp;
and propaganda, and, what is equally important,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:49.620 --> 00:26:55.320
a Parliament whose decisions really count. The&nbsp;
minorities of Czechoslovakia (unlike those of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:55.320 --> 00:27:00.660
Hungary or Yugoslavia) are allowed to form&nbsp;
political parties, the conditions regarding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:00.660 --> 00:27:05.820
which do not discriminate between the different&nbsp;
nationalities, and all of them have, in fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:05.820 --> 00:27:11.160
availed themselves of this privilege. Their&nbsp;
leaders, including those of the Magyar nationalist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:11.160 --> 00:27:17.040
party, can travel freely about the country, and&nbsp;
even abroad; can make electoral propaganda, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:17.040 --> 00:27:23.040
when they speak in Parliament or Senate at Prague&nbsp;
will be heard with due decorum, and not greeted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:23.040 --> 00:27:28.440
as they might be in neighboring capitals, with&nbsp;
ink-pots, rotten eggs, or revolver-bullets.’

00:27:28.440 --> 00:27:33.840
Despite Czechoslovakia maintaining its democratic&nbsp;
character when other countries around it were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:33.840 --> 00:27:37.920
becoming ever more authoritarian,&nbsp;
the writing was on the wall. Sudeten&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:37.920 --> 00:27:42.420
German paramilitary groups, trained,&nbsp;
funded, and supported by Nazi Germany,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:42.420 --> 00:27:46.680
started to undertake ever more drastic&nbsp;
terrorist attacks. With that said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:46.680 --> 00:27:51.840
there were Sudeten German groups that were&nbsp;
horrified by the violence and fascism and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:51.840 --> 00:27:56.640
protested against the Nazis and the Sudeten&nbsp;
Party and worked with Czechoslovak institutions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:56.640 --> 00:28:03.000
but at this point, the majority of the Sudeten&nbsp;
Germans either kept quiet or supported the Nazis.

00:28:03.000 --> 00:28:08.580
In 1938 Nazi Germany initiated a coup&nbsp;
attempt in Czechoslovakia through the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:08.580 --> 00:28:13.680
Sudeten German Party which proved unsuccessful.&nbsp;
However, it did convince the world powers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:13.680 --> 00:28:18.900
at the behest of Nazi Germany, that they had&nbsp;
to intervene and resolve the ethnic issues&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:18.900 --> 00:28:24.720
in Czechoslovakia or risk an all-out war in&nbsp;
Central Europe. This intervention resulted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:24.720 --> 00:28:29.520
in accordance with the Western appeasement&nbsp;
policy, in the Munich Agreement. Uniting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:29.520 --> 00:28:33.720
the Sudeten Germans with Germany. This&nbsp;
pretty much crippled Czechoslovakia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:33.720 --> 00:28:37.980
as the country became extremely unstable&nbsp;
politically, economically, and, most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:37.980 --> 00:28:44.400
importantly, defensively. Therefore, its takeover&nbsp;
by Nazi Germany a year later was inevitable.

00:28:44.400 --> 00:28:48.360
---------------------- WW2&nbsp;
Occupation-----------------

00:28:48.360 --> 00:28:56.220
While Slovakia became a willing de&nbsp;
facto puppet state of Nazi Germany,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:56.220 --> 00:29:00.420
Czechia was incorporated into the German&nbsp;
state as the protectorate of Bohemia and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:00.420 --> 00:29:05.340
Moravia. The entire Czech industry and people&nbsp;
were mobilised for the German war effort,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:05.340 --> 00:29:12.180
and by 1943, any civilian industry in Czechia was&nbsp;
prohibited. All Czech schools had to teach German,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:12.180 --> 00:29:17.400
Czech universities were closed, and Czech&nbsp;
intellectuals were imprisoned, shot, or sent to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:17.400 --> 00:29:23.280
labour camps. Land owned primarily by Jews, the&nbsp;
state, and enemies of the Reich was confiscated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:23.280 --> 00:29:29.220
and allocated for future German settlement.&nbsp;
A Czech Moscow informant remarked in 1941,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:29.220 --> 00:29:34.020
‘To date some hundred shot. Thousands in&nbsp;
concentration camps ... Nazis shooting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:34.020 --> 00:29:39.120
on the streets as if on hunting grounds. The&nbsp;
complete plundering of the country is horrible.’

00:29:39.120 --> 00:29:45.120
Of course, just like in any other Nazi-occupied&nbsp;
country, there were Czech Nazi collaborators,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:45.120 --> 00:29:49.920
but there was also a Czech underground&nbsp;
resistance which managed to kill Reinhard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:49.920 --> 00:29:53.880
Heydrich, the principal architect&nbsp;
of the holocaust. In retaliation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:53.880 --> 00:30:00.120
the Nazis destroyed two Czech villages suspected&nbsp;
of helping the Czech resistance. The Nazis also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:00.120 --> 00:30:04.920
instituted a crackdown on the personal&nbsp;
liberties of the Czechs in order to stifle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:04.920 --> 00:30:09.960
any future resistance. Thousands of Czechs&nbsp;
during this time were imprisoned, executed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:09.960 --> 00:30:16.200
and sent to labour camps or, more rarely, death&nbsp;
camps. It is estimated that around 36-55,000&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:17.460 --> 00:30:22.200
Czechs died in concentration camps during&nbsp;
the protectorate. All this was, of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:22.200 --> 00:30:27.780
happening alongside the holocaust, which saw&nbsp;
over ¾ of the Czech Jewish population killed.

00:30:27.780 --> 00:30:33.060
When it came to the overall German plans for&nbsp;
Czechia, they can probably be best summarised by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:33.060 --> 00:30:38.760
Hitler himself, who stated that ‘assimilation of&nbsp;
a greater part of the Czech people is possible for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:38.760 --> 00:30:44.280
historical and racial reasons, provided that those&nbsp;
Czechs who are racially useless and hostile to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:44.280 --> 00:30:50.520
Reich will be eliminated.’ How many Czechs were&nbsp;
deemed racially viable for assimilation varied&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:50.520 --> 00:30:56.280
throughout the time of the protectorate, but it&nbsp;
was usually given somewhere between 50 to 60%.

00:30:56.280 --> 00:31:01.680
The lives of Czechs in the protectorate can&nbsp;
hardly be called good, but because the Nazis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:01.680 --> 00:31:06.960
deemed much of the Czech population as racially&nbsp;
viable for assimilation, the life of an average&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:06.960 --> 00:31:12.480
Czech under Nazi rule was often better&nbsp;
than that of Poles or Ukrainians. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:12.480 --> 00:31:19.860
the Czech population even grew between 1939 and&nbsp;
1945, which can’t be said about Poland or Ukraine.

00:31:19.860 --> 00:31:25.020
When it came to Slovakia, because it had a&nbsp;
collaboration government, it did not see any harsh&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:25.020 --> 00:31:30.840
treatment by the Nazis until the unsuccessful&nbsp;
1944 Slovak National Uprising and even then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:30.840 --> 00:31:35.220
many of the Nazi-organised executions&nbsp;
in Slovakia, like those in Kremnička,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:35.220 --> 00:31:41.940
targeted mostly Jews and Roma people, not&nbsp;
Slovaks. In December of 1944, out of the estimated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:42.900 --> 00:31:49.020
13,000 people arrested by the Germans in Slovakia,&nbsp;
10,000 were Jews. Also, most of these operations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:49.020 --> 00:31:54.780
were still done in collaboration with the Slovak&nbsp;
Fascists, with the Hlinka Guard and the rest. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:54.780 --> 00:32:01.260
quote ‘German’ murders in the towns of Kremnicka&nbsp;
and Nemecká were done by both German and Fascist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:01.260 --> 00:32:06.720
Slovak soldiers. Hlinka Guard was even known to&nbsp;
be more ruthless than the Germans. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:06.720 --> 00:32:12.000
they would execute disarmed Slovak partisans&nbsp;
that the Germans released. And don’t even get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:12.000 --> 00:32:17.520
me started on the Slovak National Uprising.&nbsp;
It was a disorganised mess full of racists and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:17.520 --> 00:32:23.040
antisemites. A Jewish doctor of German-Slovak&nbsp;
heritage called Klein tried to work with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:23.040 --> 00:32:27.360
partisans because he deemed it better than&nbsp;
working with the Germans, but he reported that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:27.360 --> 00:32:33.000
‘Those Slovak [partisans] antisemites obviously&nbsp;
wanted to kill me immediately … but then the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:33.000 --> 00:32:38.520
Russians stepped up and said, don’t do it, we&nbsp;
do not have another doctor.’ Klein later added,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:38.520 --> 00:32:44.100
‘Those Slovak partisans were actually fascist&nbsp;
elements who had ended up there for all kinds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:44.100 --> 00:32:48.240
of opportunistic reasons and could not&nbsp;
handle the fact that the Jews were once&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:48.240 --> 00:32:53.580
again partners and held equal rights.’ Of&nbsp;
course, not everyone in the Slovak National&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:53.580 --> 00:32:59.580
Uprising was antisemitic and racist as it was&nbsp;
a very disorganised and heterogeneous movement,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:59.580 --> 00:33:05.340
as rebellions often are; but my god,&nbsp;
today’s official Slovak narrative of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:05.340 --> 00:33:11.940
National Uprising and of the Slovak Fascist State&nbsp;
itself, is extremely false. Anyways, where was I?

00:33:11.940 --> 00:33:17.400
Ou yes, in general, the treatment of&nbsp;
Slovaks by the German Nazis was fairly mild,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:17.400 --> 00:33:20.220
and many Slovaks, especially of the Hlinka guard,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:20.220 --> 00:33:24.960
were just as much to blame for war&nbsp;
atrocities in Slovakia as the Germans were.

00:33:24.960 --> 00:33:30.660
----------------------- ‘The Final&nbsp;
Solution’----------------------------

00:33:30.660 --> 00:33:39.300
Even before the war had ended, the&nbsp;
Czechoslovak government in exile&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:39.300 --> 00:33:44.040
and the Polish government in exile tried to&nbsp;
convince the Allied powers of the necessity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:44.040 --> 00:33:49.140
of the post-war expulsion of Germans from&nbsp;
Poland and Czechoslovakia. During this time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:49.140 --> 00:33:54.120
the Sudeten German Social Democratic party&nbsp;
was also exiled from Czechoslovakia and tried&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:54.120 --> 00:33:58.860
to work with the Czechoslovak government&nbsp;
in exile but to no avail. At this point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:58.860 --> 00:34:04.320
most Czechoslovaks viewed the Czechoslovak&nbsp;
Germans as the sole reason for the pre-war&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:04.320 --> 00:34:09.300
downfall of the country and, therefore, to try&nbsp;
to prevent any such problems in the future,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:09.300 --> 00:34:15.180
their solution was the total expulsion of the&nbsp;
entire German population from Czechoslovakia.

00:34:15.180 --> 00:34:21.300
Initially, Britain and the US were reluctant&nbsp;
to agree to a full-scale expulsion, but the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:21.300 --> 00:34:27.720
USSR wholeheartedly supported it, so when the Red&nbsp;
Army liberated Czechoslovakia between March and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:27.720 --> 00:34:33.600
May of 1945, it did not stand in the way of the&nbsp;
Czechoslovak government starting the expulsion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:33.600 --> 00:34:38.460
of the Germans. The Czechoslovak government&nbsp;
implemented a so-called ‘collective guilt’,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:38.460 --> 00:34:44.340
which stipulated that every German who did not&nbsp;
actively oppose the Nazis should be treated as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:44.340 --> 00:34:50.580
a collaborator and, therefore, expelled from the&nbsp;
country if not outright killed. The Czechoslovak&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:50.580 --> 00:34:55.980
decrees, known as the Benes decrees, did&nbsp;
stipulate that known anti-fascist Germans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:55.980 --> 00:35:01.680
German Czechoslovak collaborators, and German&nbsp;
victims of Nazi aggression would be spared,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:01.680 --> 00:35:08.160
but in reality, in the chaos of the post-war&nbsp;
expulsion, these nuances were often ignored.

00:35:08.160 --> 00:35:15.420
The Czechoslovak rhetoric around the expulsion&nbsp;
of the Germans was ironically very familiar. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:15.420 --> 00:35:20.640
Czechoslovak National Socialist party declared&nbsp;
that the primary task was ‘to clean out the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:20.640 --> 00:35:26.280
republic as a whole and completely of Germans&nbsp;
… everyone of us must help in the cleansing of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:26.280 --> 00:35:31.500
the homeland.’ Edvard Benes, the president&nbsp;
of Czechoslovakia, called for the ‘final&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:31.500 --> 00:35:36.300
solution of the German question’, and he also&nbsp;
stated that the few Germans that would remain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:36.300 --> 00:35:41.940
‘would be settled among the Czech population’&nbsp;
and ‘assimilated’. Czechoslovak officers even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:41.940 --> 00:35:48.540
forced the Germans to wear white armbands marked&nbsp;
with the letter N for Nemec, meaning German.

00:35:49.260 --> 00:35:55.920
The first few months after the red army liberated&nbsp;
Czechoslovakia were the most violent and chaotic.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:55.920 --> 00:36:01.380
The only state body that could enforce the&nbsp;
laws were Czechoslovak militias that fought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:01.380 --> 00:36:06.300
for the partisans or the red army or were&nbsp;
recently recruited from the liberated lands&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:06.300 --> 00:36:12.480
of Czechoslovakia; and many of these hastily&nbsp;
organised militias lacked officers, training,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:12.480 --> 00:36:18.720
and discipline and often acted more like roaming&nbsp;
bands of barbarians than an official state organ.

00:36:18.720 --> 00:36:24.840
‘These armed fighters drew no distinctions&nbsp;
between anti-fascist Germans, plain farmers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:24.840 --> 00:36:29.640
or Henleinist sympathisers. In a&nbsp;
paroxysm of violence that shocked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:29.640 --> 00:36:35.460
even experienced Soviet tank commanders and&nbsp;
political officers, Czechs beat up Germans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:35.460 --> 00:36:41.220
shot at them … and showed them no mercy. People&nbsp;
were randomly killed, and villages were torched&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:41.220 --> 00:36:46.740
and burned to the ground. Germans were hung&nbsp;
by their heels from trees, doused in petrol,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:46.740 --> 00:36:52.740
and set on fire … Czech militia rampaged through&nbsp;
towns and villages, shooting Germans at will.’

00:36:52.740 --> 00:36:59.220
In Usti nad Labem an accidental munition explosion&nbsp;
was erroneously blamed on the German population.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:59.220 --> 00:37:06.660
In retaliation, the Czech militia and citizens&nbsp;
rounded up, shot, and bayoneted some Germans while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:06.660 --> 00:37:12.960
others, including Women and Children, were thrown&nbsp;
off the bridge into the Elbe River and drowned. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:12.960 --> 00:37:18.480
Přerov, Carpathian Germans were passing through&nbsp;
in a train when the train was suddenly stopped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:18.480 --> 00:37:24.780
by a Czechoslovak militia. 71 men, 120 women&nbsp;
and 74 children were taken out of the train,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:24.780 --> 00:37:30.420
marched to the top of a nearby hill and were&nbsp;
forced to dig their own graves. After which,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:30.420 --> 00:37:35.640
they were all shot. When the commanding&nbsp;
officer was later questioned why he also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:35.640 --> 00:37:39.900
killed the children, he replied with, ‘Čo&nbsp;
som mal s nimi robiť, keď sme im postrieľali&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:39.900 --> 00:37:42.060
rodičov?’ (What was I supposed to do with&nbsp;
them when we already shot their parents?).

00:37:42.060 --> 00:37:48.420
Germans that weren’t killed were often at&nbsp;
gunpoint forced to leave their homes. ‘The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:48.420 --> 00:37:53.760
Germans were given very little time to pack up&nbsp;
and leave, sometimes no more than fifteen minutes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:53.760 --> 00:37:59.100
and they were allowed to take only the barest&nbsp;
minimum of things with them. They lost their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:59.100 --> 00:38:04.440
livelihoods and jobs, and they were threatened&nbsp;
with violence by the new rulers if they refused&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:04.440 --> 00:38:10.080
to vacate their homes, which were, in any&nbsp;
case, open territory for Czech plundering.’

00:38:10.080 --> 00:38:15.720
However, the infrastructure didn’t allow&nbsp;
for a simultaneous deportation of millions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:15.720 --> 00:38:21.780
of Germans living in postwar Czechoslovakia, so&nbsp;
the Germans who had to wait to be deported were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:21.780 --> 00:38:26.760
sent to Czechoslovak labour camps. These&nbsp;
labour camps were all over Czechoslovakia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:26.760 --> 00:38:31.800
and some of them were literally just repurposed&nbsp;
Nazi labour camps (Camp Teresin). The condition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:31.800 --> 00:38:38.280
in these camps was horrid, and many Germans died&nbsp;
of disease, starvation, or committed suicide.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:38.280 --> 00:38:44.520
These camps were more numerous in today’s Czechia,&nbsp;
but they also existed in Slovakia. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:44.520 --> 00:38:49.440
in the labour camp near Pezinok, several&nbsp;
Carpathian Germans, who were taken from their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:49.440 --> 00:38:54.300
homes during summer and therefore didn’t take&nbsp;
winter clothes with them, died of frostbite in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:54.300 --> 00:38:59.580
the winter when the guards refused to give them&nbsp;
any firewood or extra clothes. The Czechoslovak&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:59.580 --> 00:39:04.440
sentiments towards the Germans were made clear&nbsp;
at a different labour camp in Budejovice were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:04.440 --> 00:39:10.260
the commanding officer wrote the words ‘Eye for&nbsp;
an eye, tooth for a tooth’ on the entrance gate.

00:39:10.260 --> 00:39:16.440
Some Germans didn’t even make it to the labour&nbsp;
camps. In the infamous Death March of Brno, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:16.440 --> 00:39:21.900
entire remaining German population of the city,&nbsp;
roughly 30,000 people, were forced out of their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:21.900 --> 00:39:27.720
homes and beaten mercilessly while they marched&nbsp;
to a labour camp in Pohořelice. German estimates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:27.720 --> 00:39:33.900
put the death toll at around 2000 people during&nbsp;
the march and around another 2000 in the camp&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:33.900 --> 00:39:40.140
itself. Czech estimates state roughly 1000 deaths&nbsp;
during the march and another 1000 in the camp.

00:39:40.140 --> 00:39:44.760
Women, in particular, were treated&nbsp;
extremely poorly in these labour camps.&nbsp;

00:39:44.760 --> 00:39:49.920
‘As is often the case in ethnic cleansing,&nbsp;
the women were generally separated from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:49.920 --> 00:39:55.380
the men and sexually abused and exploited in&nbsp;
violent and obscene ways. They were constantly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:55.380 --> 00:39:59.760
called svine (“pigs”) and nacistické kurvy&nbsp;
(“nazi whores”) and sometimes were stripped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:59.760 --> 00:40:04.500
and beaten merely for the entertainment of the&nbsp;
guards.’ A woman who survived the Czechoslovak&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:04.500 --> 00:40:10.200
labour camps later recalled, ‘Every time of the&nbsp;
day we, women, were raped and our shirts torn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:10.200 --> 00:40:15.360
from our bodies’ another woman added, ‘What&nbsp;
all that happened during the raping is hard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:15.360 --> 00:40:20.460
for me to talk about. If one would imagine the&nbsp;
worst, then it remains far behind the truth.’

00:40:20.460 --> 00:40:25.500
With all this happening, it should not&nbsp;
be surprising that the suicide rate among&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:25.500 --> 00:40:31.140
the Czechoslovak Germans was extremely high.&nbsp;
General Serov, documenting the refugee crisis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:31.140 --> 00:40:36.240
happening in Germany caused by the expulsion&nbsp;
of Germans from Czechoslovakia, wrote, ‘Daily,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:36.240 --> 00:40:41.340
up to 5,000 Germans arrive in Germany from&nbsp;
Czechoslovakia, the majority of whom are women,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:41.340 --> 00:40:46.800
old folks, and children. With their futures&nbsp;
ruined and having no hope for anything better,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:46.800 --> 00:40:52.200
many of them end their lives by suicide,&nbsp;
cutting their wrists.’ On a single day in July,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:52.200 --> 00:40:57.240
in one refugee camp alone, around 71&nbsp;
Sudeten Germans committed suicide.

00:40:57.840 --> 00:41:02.880
By August of 1945, the Allied&nbsp;
powers finally agreed they would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:02.880 --> 00:41:06.720
allow the expulsion of the Germans from&nbsp;
Czechoslovakia, Poland, and Hungary,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:06.720 --> 00:41:11.280
despite the fact that it was already&nbsp;
happening for months. They declared that,

00:41:11.280 --> 00:41:15.600
‘The Three Governments, having considered&nbsp;
the question in all its aspects,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:15.600 --> 00:41:20.700
recognise that the transfer to Germany of&nbsp;
German populations, or elements thereof,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:20.700 --> 00:41:25.560
remaining in Poland, Czechoslovakia and&nbsp;
Hungary, will have to be undertaken. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:25.560 --> 00:41:31.380
agree that any transfers that take place should&nbsp;
be effected in an orderly and humane manner.’

00:41:31.380 --> 00:41:37.200
With the USA and Britain focusing heavily&nbsp;
on the ‘orderly and humane manner’ part of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:37.200 --> 00:41:43.080
this declaration. After this declaration, the&nbsp;
wild roaming bands of Czechoslovak militias&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:43.080 --> 00:41:48.600
indiscriminately purging Germans and the sudden&nbsp;
expulsion of whole cities’ German populations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:48.600 --> 00:41:53.580
like in Brno, ceased to exist, as the&nbsp;
Czechoslovak government finally created&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:53.580 --> 00:41:59.400
better systems for a more orderly conduct of&nbsp;
the expulsion. This is not to say that human&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:59.400 --> 00:42:04.800
rights violations didn’t happen, but they were on&nbsp;
a much smaller scale than in the preceding months.

00:42:04.800 --> 00:42:10.740
By 1948, out of the roughly 3.1 million&nbsp;
Germans living in Czechoslovakia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:10.740 --> 00:42:17.940
only around 165,000-200,000 remained, the&nbsp;
majority of them from mixed-ethnic families.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:17.940 --> 00:42:23.760
Despite the Czechoslovak decrees stating that&nbsp;
German anti-fascists, German Jews, and German&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:23.760 --> 00:42:30.000
Czechoslovak collaborators could stay, this often&nbsp;
was ignored. After the war, well over a thousand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:30.000 --> 00:42:35.940
Czechoslovak Jews filed complaints to the United&nbsp;
Nations for being illegally deported. People like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:35.940 --> 00:42:41.760
Hilda Elsner, who had to do forced labour for the&nbsp;
Nazis and whose father was killed in a death camp,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:41.760 --> 00:42:46.980
were deported by the Czechoslovaks simply because&nbsp;
they didn’t speak Czechoslovak. In another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:46.980 --> 00:42:52.500
incident of illegal deportation of anti-fascist&nbsp;
Germans in Pezinok, a Czechoslovak official&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:52.500 --> 00:42:57.680
stated, ‘v Ríši môžu preukázať cenné služby, a u&nbsp;
nás bude aspoň menší počet Nemcov’ (‘in the Reich&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:57.680 --> 00:42:59.340
they can provide valuable services and we will at&nbsp;
least end up with a smaller number of Germans’).

00:42:59.340 --> 00:43:04.740
The facts that some Czechoslovak Germans&nbsp;
actively protested against the German Sudeten&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:04.740 --> 00:43:10.740
Party in the 1930s or that, during the war,&nbsp;
the Sudeten German Social Democrats in exile&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:10.740 --> 00:43:14.340
tried to collaborate with the Czechoslovak&nbsp;
government, or that there were plenty of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:14.340 --> 00:43:20.760
German Jews or anti-fascist Germans that resisted&nbsp;
the Nazis, were all ignored. For the most part,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:20.760 --> 00:43:25.320
if you only spoke German, you were&nbsp;
expelled from the country, if not worse.

00:43:25.320 --> 00:43:31.680
All the wealth which Germans were forced to&nbsp;
leave behind was redistributed to Czechoslovaks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:31.680 --> 00:43:35.700
and Czechoslovak colonisations of&nbsp;
the now-depopulated areas were highly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:35.700 --> 00:43:42.300
promoted with free land giveaways, tax cuts, and&nbsp;
business handouts. Despite this vigorous promotion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:42.300 --> 00:43:47.940
of colonisation by the state, the depopulated&nbsp;
areas still suffered extreme labour shortages,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:47.940 --> 00:43:53.340
and in certain areas, Hungarians from southern&nbsp;
Czechoslovakia had to be forcibly resettled to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:53.340 --> 00:43:58.380
shore up these labour shortages. Due to the&nbsp;
lack of human capital, production in these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:58.380 --> 00:44:04.380
depopulated areas drastically fell. In the Little&nbsp;
Carpathians, there were wine and lumber shortages&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:04.380 --> 00:44:09.660
because it was the Germans who were winemakers&nbsp;
and lumberjacks, and the Czechoslovaks that were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:09.660 --> 00:44:15.540
given these new jobs weren’t trained. Factories,&nbsp;
which relied heavily on trained labour, suffered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:15.540 --> 00:44:21.720
the most. As Gerlach points out, ‘In many cases&nbsp;
Germans possessed skills and experience vital to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:21.720 --> 00:44:27.060
sustain production in local factories’, experience&nbsp;
that was now completely gone from the country.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:27.060 --> 00:44:33.720
Higher skilled jobs in the Sudetenland, like&nbsp;
finances, saw a 26% decrease, health care 21%,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:33.720 --> 00:44:40.440
and the number of people with university degrees&nbsp;
fell by 22.2%. By killing and deporting most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:40.440 --> 00:44:45.600
of its German population, Czechoslovakia&nbsp;
drastically hindered its post-war economy.

00:44:46.320 --> 00:44:50.640
The deportation of almost the entire&nbsp;
German population of Czechoslovakia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:50.640 --> 00:44:56.220
whether we like it or not, was purely an act&nbsp;
of the Czechoslovak people and our government.

00:44:58.940 --> 00:44:59.940
-------Conclusion------

00:44:59.940 --> 00:45:05.460
The history of the German-speaking population&nbsp;
of Czechoslovakia is very complicated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:05.460 --> 00:45:11.880
and in this video, we touched upon only its last&nbsp;
chapter. It was a remnant of a medieval past&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:11.880 --> 00:45:17.580
when ethnic affiliations did not matter as much,&nbsp;
resulting in the creation of multiethnic kingdoms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:17.580 --> 00:45:22.740
like that of Bohemia and Austria. However,&nbsp;
as nationalism took hold in Europe and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:22.740 --> 00:45:28.080
idea of an ethnocentric nation-state became&nbsp;
the norm- gradually increasing nationalistic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:28.080 --> 00:45:33.540
fanaticism developed on both sides, which&nbsp;
culminated ‘in one of the largest waves of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:33.540 --> 00:45:38.280
forced migration in history’. With no one ever&nbsp;
stopping to ask whether an ‘eye for an eye’&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:38.280 --> 00:45:41.704
won’t leave the whole country blind.
Jelikož jsem měl k videu slovenské&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:41.704 --> 00:45:42.212
titulky a citoval jsem v něm několik slovenských&nbsp;
pasáží, rozhodl jsem se pro zábavu udělat outro&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:42.211 --> 00:45:42.753
v češtině. Rozhodně mám divný přízvuk, ale hej,&nbsp;
alespoň mě to přijde vtipný. Každopádně doufám,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:42.753 --> 00:45:43.280
že se vám video líbilo a dozvěděli jste se něco&nbsp;
nového. Samozřejmě jsem o tomto tématu mohl říct&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:43.280 --> 00:45:43.797
mnohem více než se vešlo do tohoto videa, takže&nbsp;
pokud se chcete o tématu dozvědět více, podívejte&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:43.797 --> 00:45:44.300
se na bibliografii, link je v připnutém komentáři.&nbsp;
Tohle byla velmi těžká a kontroverzní téma,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:44.300 --> 00:45:44.721
a jsem si jistý, že o tom nedostanu žádné&nbsp;
rozzlobené e-maily. /s Pravděpodobně&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:44.721 --> 00:45:45.248
dostanu více rozzlobených e-mailů o tomto outru,&nbsp;
který se snažím udělat v češtině. Také si nejsem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:45.248 --> 00:45:45.751
jistý, zda je toto video zmonetizovano. Musel jsem&nbsp;
upravit určité části videa, abych se ho pokusil&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:45.751 --> 00:45:46.254
zmonetisovat, ale pokud stále není zmonetizovano,&nbsp;
